[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Consecutive Calls and thenAnswer() in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing unit tests, you often encounter scenarios where a mock method needs to return different values on successive calls, or execute custom logic based on the arguments passed to it. Mockito provides powerful features for these use cases: consecutive stubbing and the thenAnswer() method."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Consecutive Calls"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consecutive calls allow you to define a sequence of behaviors for a mocked method. When the method is invoked multiple times in your test, Mockito returns the configured values in the exact order they were defined."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"when(mockService.getData()).thenReturn("First Call", "Second Call", "Third Call");"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alternatively, you can chain the thenReturn() calls to achieve the exact same behavior."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"when(mockService.getData()).thenReturn("First Call").thenReturn("Second Call").thenReturn("Third Call");"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Using thenAnswer() for Dynamic Behavior"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes returning static values is not enough. You might need to inspect the arguments passed to the mock method and compute a response dynamically. This is where thenAnswer() comes into play, utilizing the Answer interface."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"when(mockCalculator.add(anyInt(), anyInt())).thenAnswer(invocation -> {
    int arg1 = invocation.getArgument(0);
    int arg2 = invocation.getArgument(1);
    return arg1 + arg2;
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Combining Consecutive Calls with thenAnswer()"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also chain thenAnswer() and thenReturn() consecutively to handle complex test scenarios where the first few calls return static data, but subsequent calls require dynamic computation."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"when(mockService.process(anyString()))
    .thenReturn("Cached Result")
    .thenAnswer(invocation -> {
        String arg = invocation.getArgument(0);
        return "Processed: " + arg;
    });"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use consecutive calls when testing state transitions or retry mechanisms."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use thenAnswer() when you need to mock complex logic, inspect arguments, or simulate side effects."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your stubbing logic readable and avoid overly complex thenAnswer implementations inside your tests."}]
